  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/8/8c/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_98.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Don Blake/Thor is steamed about Jane Foster leaving him to work for Dr. Andrews, letting off steam in his medical office.&nbsp; It's all because of <i>Odin</i> that he could not tell Jane that Thor and Don Blake are one and the same -- he <i>forbade</i> him from confessing his feelings of love toward a... <i>mortal</i>!\n\nAt that moment, Odin himself summons his son to Asgard, and there urges his son to \"put all thoughts of the human Jane Foster from out of thy mind!\"&nbsp; After only two panels, Thor turns his back and leaves, saying that \"Even the God of Thunder cannot close his heart upon command!\"&nbsp; Returning home, a heartsick Dr. Blake then decides to head out East to get away from his sorrow.\n\nAt the same time, in a \"lonely, isolated region of India,\" a Professor Shecktor is closing in on finding an antidote for snake venom.&nbsp; Realizing that he'll share in <i>none</i> of the credit for the discovery, his jealous assistant, Klaus (who we later learn is an ex-convict), stages an accident -- allowing a cobra to bite them both and withholding the antidote from the professor.\n\nNews of the \"accident\" soon reaches Bombay, where Don Blake is travelling, and the doctor soon overhears that Professor Shecktor is dying -- that's his old <i>teacher!</i>  He decides to step in as Thor to try and help.&nbsp; He learns from the dying professor that his assistant actually <i>plotted</i> his murder, withholding the antidote from him, all the while not realizing that he (the Professor) had been experimenting on the snake -- it was a <i>radioactive cobra</i>!&nbsp; As such, the antidote affected him -- changed him and gave him the abilities and instincts of a cobra!\n\nThor is too late to save the life of the Professor, but he vows to track down the Cobra, \"and <i>destroy</i> him!\"&nbsp; In fact, he finds that the thrill of the hunt has reinvigorated him -- \"the menace of a deadly foe\" and promise of danger and high adventure -- this is <i>exactly</i> what he needs to get his mind off Jane.\n\nThor soon learns that the Cobra has boarded a jet bound for the United States, and with the help of a summoned typhoon, he is able to get to Idlewild Airport before the Cobra, watching him slither up the walls, deciding to follow him and learn his plans before ultimately striking.\n\nCobra heads to a chemical manufacturing plant and demands complete control so he can make gallons of the cobra serum \"to create a vast army of others like myself -- to do my bidding and serve me without question!\"\n\nThor then bursts in, and we get to see a handful of Cobra's interesting abilities to hurl darts and venom in the form of gas.&nbsp; Eventually, Cobra tries to blast him with a huge rush of chemical acid-based formula, but it only enrages Thor, and at the bottom of page 9, we see Thor release a \"blood-curdling war cry\" and hurl his hammer, which misses the slippery Cobra, but crashes into the vat of gas, and its vapors blind him just long enough to allow Cobra to escape.\n\n<b>FIXME FIXME</b>\n\nHe heads out to find a doctor's office where he might find \"serums with which I can achieve my ultimate objective!\"&nbsp; Sure enough, it's the office of Dr. Andrews, who is there with his new nurse, Jane Foster!&nbsp; Dr. Andrews immediately caves to the Cobra's demands, but Jane Foster is incensed that he seems to show no backbone at all - he's nothing but a coward!\n\nSeeing Thor flying outside, she hurls something through the window to attract his attention, and the Cobra prepares to kill her for her insolence!&nbsp; But Thor has taken note and arrives.&nbsp; Cobra grabs the girl as a hostage and heads out the window toward the nearby bridge.&nbsp; But Thor quickly finds a way to subdue Cobra and save Jane, who immediately leaves Dr. Andrews and heads back to ask Don Blake for her old job back.\n\nThe narrative text implies that it might have been Odin's doing that drew Jane Foster back to Don Blake - \"out of love for his favorite son!\"&nbsp; The pair are together again!&nbsp; However the Cobra has escaped!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "The story itself is mundane, the villain derivative (in the sense that his powers stem from being bitten by a \"radioactive cobra\"), and the fact that Jane Foster is literally gone from the presence of Dr. Blake for a <i>mere one issue</i> -- all are marks against this offering.\n\n[ASSET: left | CreateAnArmy | width=19%] Klaus, the ex-convict assistant of Professor Shecktor, who accidentally finds himself affected by the bite of a radioactive cobra, seems so confident in his powers of persuasion that he immediately heads back to the US to order the board of a chemical manufacturing plant to create <i>gallons</i> of cobra serum so that he can create an army, one that will \"do my bidding and serve me without question!\"\n\nNot only does this strike me as a foolhardy plan, it sounds a <i>lot</i> like what the Lizard was planning in last month's <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> ([LINK:1963:ASM_6|ASM #6]), only with <b>cobras</b>, rather than reptiles (which actually <i>included</i> snakes -- see its page 15).&nbsp; So in one issue, plotter Stan Lee has not only copied Spider-Man's origin, he's followed it up with the same evil plan as last month's villain.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=\"Derivative\" doesn't even <i>begin</i> to describe such a dastardly scheme!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | OnlyIfThrown | width=19%] [ASSET: right | FallenHammer | width=21%] Naturally, the issue provides a relatively straight-forward battle sequence (pages 7-9), during which time, the Cobra ensnares Thor in his \"unbreakable cobra-cord, causing Thor to momentarily drop his hammer in surprise, and it is at that point that mentally notes that his hammer only returns to him if actually thrown.&nbsp; But if only dropped, it remains motionless until retrieved.\n[BR]\n[BR] At the end of page 8, when he goes back to retrieve it (now as Don Blake), he \"stamps it once upon the ground\" to presumably turn back to Thor.&nbsp; While the fact that the fallen weapon is still a hammer is consistent with the content of previous issues, the fact that he must stamp it on the ground is new.&nbsp; Remember back in [LINK:1962:JIM_83_thor0|JIM #83] (page 7), he only needed to <i>touch </i>the hammer to resume his Thor persona.&nbsp; (An apparent discrepancy to keep an eye out for in the future.)\n\n\n[ASSET: left | ThorInBattle | width=16%] [SPAN: severity=success; text=On the positive side was that one <i>awesome</i> panel that closes out page 9.]\n[BR]\n[BR] As he hurls his hammer, Thor shouts out \"By the beard of noble Odin, oh trusty hammer - <i>strike</i>!&nbsp; Strike in the name of <i>Asgard</i>!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] While the panel isn't drawn so wonderfully by artist Don Heck and will never rank in the upper echelon of even mildly famous panels, [SPAN: severity=info; text=it nevertheless captures the Thunder God in a moment of glory, relishing the prospect of battle!]\n[BR]\n[BR] Seeing Thor basking in the thrill of the hunt is truly one of the most invigorating scenes this issue brings to the table!&nbsp; It portrays him (at last) as more than just a powerful figure -- it transforms him into a <b><i>warrior</i></b>, one born to do battle, as if he's finally embraced what he is.\n\nIs it a shame that it took a third-tier villain like the Cobra to wake up the slumbering God of Thunder?&nbsp; <i>Sure</i>.\n[BR] Nevertheless, even though the panel isn't good enough to pin up on your wall, it still is the first I've seen so far of Thor spouting off such an outburst, so I find it notable.\n\nWhat's also interesting is that Thor strikes \"in the name of Asgard,\" even though Odin's decrees about his interactions with Jane are a large part of his problems.&nbsp; Without realizing it ([SPAN: severity=warning; text=and perhaps this was Odin's plan all along]), it comes across as a brand new persona that swelling inside him.&nbsp; Even his <i>speech</i> seems more formal, a style reminiscent of the King James translation of the Bible or a William Shakespeare play (both originating in the same general time period, that being the early 1600s).\n\nThis is the first time I recall Thor making such an outburst, and indeed, on multiple occasions in this issue, Thor <i>clearly</i> is reveling in the conflict (e.g. page 5, panel 5, and the first panel on page 9).&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: I must point out that my first exposure to Thor was in <i>Avengers</i> 162, where we see him cry out \"Death to Ultron!&nbsp; Verily, this night we shall <i>avenge</i> our slain comrades, or taste death's bitter cup ourselves!\"&nbsp; Now <i>that's </i>the Thor I know!&nbsp; So I'm thrilled to see the first hint of that here in this issue!]&nbsp; It's as if he's <i>thrilled</i> to be in action, once more pitted against an evil foe (even one so mundane as the Cobra).\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\nThat panel -- and Thor thinking in passing on at least two occasions how great it is to find himself pitted against an evil foe -- both serve to push the final grade on this one up a full point to just above \"reader\" level (and another half-point for the <i>Tales of Asgard</i> offering as well -- see below -- still not mid-grade).&nbsp; Cobra himself seems an unworthy foe for someone like Thor, but this one single shining moment (complete with battle cry) in an otherwise unremarkable tale made it completely worth the read!\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nA couple of side notes:\n\n\nWhen Don Blake calls Professor Shecktor his old teacher, this implies a past for Blake.&nbsp; Interesting since we find out later that the Blake persona was a made-up one by Odin.&nbsp; Will tuck this tidbit aside for comparison later.",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>Tales of Asgard </b>introduces us to Ymir, King of the Ice Giants! (and here Jack Kirby once again is handling the artwork, as opposed to Don Heck in the main story)\n\nWe see Odin in pitched combat against a group of ice giants, but with the aid of his \"magic sword,\" he hurls them into a chasm, plunging them to \"Surtur's fiery domain!\"&nbsp; But one ice giant remains - Ymir!&nbsp; Odin's speech is full-on Asgardian, complete with the wordy outbursts we've come to expect from Thor, and it's actually quite invigorating reading it. J  \"While breath remains in me, I shall fight you!&nbsp; And by my beard, the victory shall yet be mine!\"&nbsp; (This is good stuff!)&nbsp; Odin surrounds him in a ring of fire, trapping him, and returns to his throne!\n\nNext issue, we're promised Surtur, the Fire Demon!\n\nUnlike the initial Tales of Asgard, which was largely painting a broad history, this issue actually shows Odin in action, and I sense that Stan Lee was slowly imbuing these Asgardians with a regal quality, particularly in their speech patterns.&nbsp; The narrative very much hinted that he was really getting into this style of writing.&nbsp; I very much suspect that we will soon start seeing this bleed over into the main Thor stories themselves....\n\nBottom Line: a short and mildly entertaining look at an early adventure of Odin.&nbsp; Nice to have some background material to help embellish the mythos!\n\n<b>Perhaps this book is turning the corner.&nbsp; There's certainly a hint of that here!</b>"
  ],
  "ownership": "I was fortunate enough to get a FN+ or so copy that came to me as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $18, and eventually sold on eBay in Feb 2020 for $99, netting me over $68.\n[DEVNOTE: I had another copy which I have kept.]",
